{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Wu2018-planar-ITO-aSi-Ag-reference",
  "Source": "J. Wu, \"Absorption enhancement in thin-film solar cells based on periodically chirped structure,\" Sol. Energy 165, 85-89 (2018). doi:10.1016/j.solener.2018.03.004 - Text p. 86 (Sec. 2, last paragraph, and Fig. 1(b)); ITO thickness h_a = 60 nm from Sec. 3",
  "Notes": "Planar reference thin-film a-Si solar cell: ITO antireflection layer on an a-Si absorber backed by an Ag reflector (absorption in the a-Si is the figure of merit)\nSpecification: Maximize AM1.5-weighted integrated absorption in the a-Si over 300-800 nm (Eq. 1); short-circuit current density Jsc over 300-800 nm with unit quantum efficiency (Eq. 3).\nReported: Absorption of the a-Si in the planar reference is > 60 % from 344 to 671 nm (Fig. 2a), low in the short and long wavebands; average integrated absorption ~71 % and Jsc 18.03 mA/cm^2 at normal incidence (AM1.5-weighted, 300-800 nm). Integrated absorption > 60 % for incident angles up to 66 deg (TE) and 69 deg (TM); Jsc > 16 mA/cm^2 up to 60 deg (TE) and 66 deg (TM).\nNotes: Paper lists the stack top to bottom (ITO / a-Si / Ag), same as here. The Ag back reflector is 200 nm thick, i.e. optically opaque, so it is treated as the substrate. ITO is given only as n = 2 (no k). a-Si and Ag optical constants are from refractiveindex.info (CRYSTALS group, a-Si) with no numbers in the paper; the names 'a-Si' and 'Ag' need a library dispersion. 'Optimized' ITO thickness 60 nm is stated together with the grating parameters; the text for the planar cell says 'an optimized antireflection coating (ITO) with a thickness h_a of 60 nm' and a-Si slab 'thickness h1 of 150 nm'. The figure of merit is absorption in the a-Si only (not total 1-R), weighted by the AM1.5G spectrum, which a plain AA operand does not reproduce exactly; reference_nm 550 is arbitrary (not given). Reported numbers mix TE/TM average; for the planar stack TE and TM coincide at normal incidence.\nLayers from Text p. 86 (Sec. 2, last paragraph, and Fig. 1(b)); ITO thickness h_a = 60 nm from Sec. 3; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), compare with the paper: ours A (all layers) > 60 % from 351 to 690 nm; mean A 300-800 nm 74.6 %; paper a-Si absorption > 60 % from 344 to 671 nm; AM1.5-weighted average 71 %. AA here is absorption in every layer (ITO and Ag too); the paper counts the a-Si alone, so ours is the upper bound.",
